{"name":"Chapel Choir","hex":"#bbd1e2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chapel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place of worship, smaller than or subordinate to a church.","A place of worship in another building or within a civil institution such as a larger church, airport, prison, monastery, school, etc.; often primarily for private prayer."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Describing a person who attends a nonconformist chapel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chapel"},{"term":"choir","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A group of people who sing together; a company of people who are trained to sing together.","One quarter of a cruciform church, or the architectural area of a church, generally used by the choir; often near the apse."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To sing in concert."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/choir"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chapel","blocks":[{"text":"A chapel is a Christian place of prayer and worship that is usually relatively small. The term has several meanings. First, smaller spaces inside a church that have their own altar are often called chapels; the Lady chapel is a common type of these. Second, a chapel is a place of worship, sometimes interfaith, that is part of a building, complex, or vessel with some other main purpose, such as a school, college, hospital, palace or large aristocratic house, castle, barracks, prison, funeral home, cemetery, hotel, airport, or military or commercial ship. Third, chapels are small places of worship, built as satellite sites by a church or monastery, for example in remote areas; these are often called a chapel of ease. A feature of all these types is that often no clergy are permanently resident or specifically attached to the chapel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/St_Paul%27s_Cathedral_Chapel_of_St_Michael_%26_St_George%2C_London_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-St_Paul%27s_Cathedral_Chapel_of_St_Michael_%26_St_George%2C_London_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapel","wikidata":"Q108325"},{"title":"Choir","blocks":[{"text":"A choir, also known as a chorale or chorus, is a musical ensemble of singers. Choral music, in turn, is the music written specifically for such an ensemble to perform or in other words is the music performed by the ensemble. Choirs may perform music from the classical music repertoire, which spans from the medieval era to the present, or popular music repertoire. Most choirs are led by a conductor, who leads the performances with arm, hand, and facial gestures."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Evensong_in_York_Minster.jpg/330px-Evensong_in_York_Minster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choir","wikidata":"Q131186"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Apsidal_chapels.png/500px-Apsidal_chapels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Apsidal_chapels.png/1280px-Apsidal_chapels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Apsidal_chapels.png/500px-Apsidal_chapels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Schematic rendering of typical \"side chapels\" in the apse of a cathedral, surrounding the ambulatory","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AApsidal_chapels.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG/500px-Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG/1280px-Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG/500px-Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Tsrviz Chapel in Armenia, one of the oldest chapels in the world","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Chapelle_Palatine.jpg/500px-Chapelle_Palatine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Chapelle_Palatine.jpg/500px-Chapelle_Palatine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Cappella Palatina in Palermo, Italy (pictured), and the Palatine Chapel in Aachen, two of the most famous palace chapels of Europe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChapelle_Palatine.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg/500px-Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg/1280px-Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg/500px-Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Capel Salem, a nonconformist chapel in Pwllheli, Wales. Unlike earlier types of chapel, this chapel is not attached to a larger place of worship.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACapel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Christ_the_King_Chapel_%28Christendom_College%29_-_2.jpg/500px-Christ_the_King_